package "pkg/binate/native/arm32"

import "pkg/binate/asm"
import "pkg/binate/asm/arm32"
import "pkg/binate/ir"
import "pkg/binate/native/common"
import "pkg/binate/types"

// Native arm32 emission for the SMALL-single-aggregate PACK-RESULT path of a
// capturing closure / method-value shim (Phase C.2) —
// the sibling of the C.1 big-single-aggregate SRET path
// (arm32_closure_shim_aggregate.bn).  Split out to stay under the hygiene file-length
// cap.  Reuses the arm32 NON-closure spill machinery (emitSpillMarshalArm32 +
// emitClosureCaptureSpillArm32 / emitClosureCaptureRegLoadSpillArm32) with a CAPTURE
// (no-sret-prefix) classify, because the underlying takes NO retbuf arg — it returns
// the <=4-byte aggregate BY VALUE in R0.
//
// The shim is entered under the aggregate-return dispatch ABI (emitCallFuncValue):
//
//     R0 = retbuf   (address of the caller's result buffer)
//     R1 = data     (the closure-struct pointer)
//     R2.. = user args
//
// The underlying lifted body's REAL ABI is `underlying(captures…, users…)`: it takes
// NO retbuf (a <=4-byte aggregate is returned by value in R0), so captures classify
// DENSELY from R0 (classifyOff = 0, regBase = 0 — the same origin the SCALAR closure
// shim uses) and the users follow.  So — unlike C.1 sret — the shim runs a FRAMED BL
// (not a tail-branch): it saves the retbuf, marshals captures + users, BLs the
// underlying, then stores the returned R0 word through the retbuf.
//
// THE CRUX (why pack differs from C.1 sret).  For sret the capture base is R1 and
// capture 0 classifies at R1 (past the R0 retbuf slot), so capture 0's DESTINATION ==
// the base register, and the right-to-left load keeps R1 live until capture 0 loads
// last.  For PACK that does NOT hold: captures classify from R0, so capture 0's
// destination is R0 but the data base is entry R1.  A capture at classify-index 1
// targets R1 and — under the right-to-left capture load — loads BEFORE capture 0,
// which would CLOBBER the R1 data base before capture 0 (targeting R0) reads its field
// from [R1].  Worse, the user marshal writes R(NumCaptureParams)..; when there is 1
// capture the users start at R1, so the marshal too would overwrite the R1 data base.
// Either is a SILENT MISCOMPILE.
//
// CLOBBER-SAFE BASE.  The shim stages every incoming GP dispatch reg (r0..r3) to the
// frame, then RE-HOMES the data pointer into R5 (a callee-saved register the shim
// saves in its entry push) by loading it from the staged data slot.  R5 is distinct
// from R4 (the capture-spill memory→memory shuttle / the post-BL retbuf holder) and is
// never a capture-load or user-marshal destination (captures fill R0.., users follow,
// both scratch through R4 / IP only), so it survives from the staged-slot load through
// the final capture register load.  Every capture read then addresses [R5 + FieldOffset]
// — immune to the R1 / R0.. clobbers above.  This is the task's "re-home the base to a
// callee-saved register" option; it costs one extra saved register (R5) over the C.1
// sret spill's push, resolved by widening the entry push to {r4, r5, r6, lr} (16 bytes,
// 8-aligned — R6 is unused, present only to keep the push an even register count).
//
// RETBUF-SAVE REGISTER PRESSURE.  The non-closure pack (emitPackShim) parks the retbuf
// in R4 across the whole shim.  Here R4 is ALSO the capture-spill shuttle (a stack-bound
// capture word loads to R4, then stores to the outgoing stack), so R4 cannot hold the
// retbuf across the marshal.  The retbuf is instead STASHED to a dedicated frame slot
// (retbufOff) and reloaded into R4 only AFTER the BL — exactly the non-closure PACK
// SPILL's split (emitFuncvalSpillShimArm32's usePack arm) — so R4 is free as the shuttle
// during the marshal and holds the retbuf only for the single post-BL store.

// emitClosureShimPackArm32 emits the small-single-aggregate PACK capturing-closure /
// method-value shim (Phase C.2).  argTypes is the outgoing captures++users type list
// the caller (emitClosureShimArm32) already built.  Delegates to the shared framing
// core with retTuple = nil (the underlying returns the <=4-byte aggregate BY VALUE in
// R0, stored as one word through the retbuf).
func emitClosureShimPackArm32(a @asm.Assembler, fnLabel @[]char, fvTyp @types.Type, f @ir.Func,
		cc common.CallConv, argTypes @[]@types.Type) {
	emitClosureShimPackCoreArm32(a, fnLabel, fvTyp, f, cc, argTypes, nil)
}

// emitClosureShimPackCoreArm32 is the shared framed-BL PACK-framing core for both the
// SMALL-single-aggregate PACK result (C.2 — retTuple = nil, the underlying returns the
// <=4-byte aggregate BY VALUE in R0) and the SMALL MULTI-RETURN result (C.3 — retTuple =
// the multi-return tuple, the underlying returns the tuple FIELD-PER-REGISTER in r0..r3).
// The two shapes share EVERYTHING up to the BL — the R5-re-home capture base (THE CRUX),
// the retbuf stash, the capture + user marshal, the widened {r4,r5,r6,lr} frame — and
// differ ONLY in the post-BL store through the reloaded-into-R4 retbuf:
//   - retTuple == nil: a single STR R0 (the by-value aggregate word).
//   - retTuple != nil: storeMultiReturnTupleFieldsArm32(R4, retTuple, …) — the same
//     field-per-register-by-class store the callee's return lowering (emitMultiReturnPack)
//     and the non-closure small-multiret shim (emitMultiRetPackShim) use, so the closure
//     shim and the callee provably agree on r0..r3 → retbuf-field placement.
// The shim is ALWAYS framed (a pack / small-multiret return needs a real BL — the
// underlying returns the result in registers, which the shim stores through the retbuf),
// and ALWAYS stages r0..r3 so the R5-based capture reads are clobber-safe (see THE CRUX).
// Register-fit and over-budget captures/users share this one path: the classifier places
// each capture/user at its authoritative R0.. reg or outgoing-stack slot, so a capture
// even-pair pad, a SPLIT capture, and an over-budget user arg are all handled by the
// shared spill helpers — no separate frameless path (a pack shim can never tail-branch).
func emitClosureShimPackCoreArm32(a @asm.Assembler, fnLabel @[]char, fvTyp @types.Type, f @ir.Func,
		cc common.CallConv, argTypes @[]@types.Type, retTuple @types.Type) {
	// outBytes = the outgoing-args area for the FULL captures++users call (a stack-bound
	// capture's SPLIT tail and an over-budget user arg spill).  Captures classify DENSELY
	// from R0 (no retbuf/data prefix — the underlying returns by value, taking no sret
	// arg), so the classify list is argTypes as-is (no prependSretPtr, unlike C.1).
	// CallStackBytes already rounds to StackAlign (8).
	var outBytes int = cc.CallStackBytes(argTypes)
	var savedBytes int = 16
	// Inner layout (low→high, all below the {r4,r5,r6,lr} save):
	//   [0, outBytes)                        outgoing-args (8-rounded already)
	//   [retbufOff, +WordBytes)              retbuf save (reloaded post-BL)
	//   [stageOff, +NumGpArgRegs*WordBytes)  clobber-safe staging of r0..r3
	var retbufOff int = outBytes
	var stageOff int = outBytes + cc.WordBytes
	var innerBytes int = stageOff + cc.NumGpArgRegs * cc.WordBytes
	if innerBytes % cc.StackAlign != 0 {
		innerBytes = innerBytes + (cc.StackAlign - innerBytes % cc.StackAlign)
	}
	// frameDelta = the SP decrement from shim entry ({r4,r5,r6,lr} save + inner area),
	// used by the spill marshal to address the dispatch caller's incoming-overflow words.
	var frameDelta int = savedBytes + innerBytes

	// push {r4, r5, r6, lr}: save the caller's R4 (memory→memory shuttle + post-BL
	// retbuf holder), R5 (the re-homed data base), R6 (unused — even-count alignment
	// padding), and LR (the BL clobbers it).  Four registers keep SP 8-aligned (AAPCS32).
	arm32.Push(a, arm32.COND_AL, packClosurePushMask())
	if innerBytes > 0 {
		emitSpAdjust(a, false, innerBytes)
	}
	// Stage r0..r3 before any outgoing write; the user marshal reads register-source
	// words from [sp + stageOff + WordBytes*g], and the data base is re-homed from the
	// staged data slot below.  emitFrameStore (not a raw STR) materializes a large stageOff
	// into IP (argReg(g) is R0..R3, never IP — no aliasing).
	for g := 0; g < cc.NumGpArgRegs; g++ {
		emitFrameStore(a, argReg(g), stageOff + cc.WordBytes * g)
	}
	// Stash the retbuf (staged at g=0) to its dedicated slot; reloaded into R4 post-BL.
	// R0 is still live here (nothing has overwritten it yet), so store it directly.
	emitFrameStore(a, arm32.R0, retbufOff)
	// Re-home the data pointer (entry R1, staged at g=1) into R5 — the clobber-safe
	// capture base (see THE CRUX).  R5 is untouched by the marshal and capture loads
	// below, so this one load carries the base through to the final capture register load.
	emitFrameLoad(a, arm32.R5, stageOff + cc.WordBytes)
	// Step: spill stack-bound capture words from the re-homed base (R5), classified over
	// argTypes at index i (classifyOff = 0 — no sret prefix).  The shuttle is R4 (freed
	// for reuse as the post-BL retbuf holder), the address scratch IP.
	emitClosureCaptureSpillArm32(a, cc, f, argTypes, 0, arm32.R5)
	// Step: marshal the user args over the CAPTURE-PREFIX classify (argTypes =
	// captures++users, classifyBase = NumCaptureParams, srcPrefix = 2 drops retbuf +
	// data).  staged = true (every incoming word was staged above).
	var userTypes @[]@types.Type = shimUserTypesArm32(fvTyp)
	emitSpillMarshalArm32(a, cc, argTypes, f.NumCaptureParams, userTypes, 2, frameDelta, stageOff,
			true)
	// Step: load register-bound capture words from R5 into their outgoing regs, right-to-
	// left, classified over argTypes at index i (classifyOff = 0).  The base is R5 (NOT
	// R0/R1), so capture 1 targeting R1 no longer clobbers the base before capture 0's
	// [R5 + 0] read (THE CRUX).
	emitClosureCaptureRegLoadSpillArm32(a, cc, f, argTypes, 0, arm32.R5)
	// Step: BL the underlying.  For a single aggregate it returns the <=4-byte
	// aggregate by value in R0; for a small multi-return it returns the tuple
	// field-per-register in r0..r3.
	a.SetGlobal(fnLabel)
	arm32.Bl(a, arm32.COND_AL, fnLabel)
	// Reload the stashed retbuf into R4 (R4 was the marshal shuttle, free post-BL) and
	// store the returned register(s) through it.  Single aggregate: one STR R0 (the
	// by-value word).  Small multi-return: storeMultiReturnTupleFieldsArm32 stores each
	// tuple field from its return register r0..rN through the retbuf at the field's
	// offset — the SAME per-field-by-class store the callee's return lowering emits, so
	// the two agree on the r0..r3 → retbuf-field convention.  IP is the large-offset
	// scratch, unused here (a tuple's own field offsets fit the STR immediate).  A
	// 0-byte single-aggregate result never reaches this pack path: it is void-like
	// (types.IsAggregateReturnTyp false), so the closure shim dispatch
	// (shimReturnIsSmallPackAggregateArm32) routes it to the scalar/void tail-branch
	// instead — the single aggregate here therefore always has a byte to store.
	emitFrameLoad(a, arm32.R4, retbufOff)
	if retTuple == nil {
		arm32.Str(a, arm32.COND_AL, arm32.R0, arm32.MemImm(arm32.R4, 0))
	} else {
		storeMultiReturnTupleFieldsArm32(a, retTuple, arm32.R4, 0, cc.NumGpRetRegs, cc.WordBytes,
				arm32.IP)
	}
	// Tear down the frame and return.
	if innerBytes > 0 {
		emitSpAdjust(a, true, innerBytes)
	}
	arm32.Pop(a, arm32.COND_AL, packClosurePopMask())
}

// packClosurePushMask is the {r4, r5, r6, lr} register mask (bit i = register i) for
// the pack closure shim's entry push: save the caller's R4 (memory→memory shuttle +
// post-BL retbuf holder), R5 (the re-homed clobber-safe data base), R6 (unused — even-
// count alignment padding to keep SP 8-aligned across the push), and LR (the BL clobbers
// it).  Wider than the scalar/sret closure spill's {r4, lr} because pack needs the
// extra callee-saved R5 for the data base (see THE CRUX in the file header).
func packClosurePushMask() int {
	return (1 << arm32.R4) | (1 << arm32.R5) | (1 << arm32.R6) | (1 << arm32.LR)
}

// packClosurePopMask is the {r4, r5, r6, pc} register mask for the pack closure shim's
// exit pop: restore the caller's R4 / R5 / R6 and load the saved LR into PC to return.
func packClosurePopMask() int {
	return (1 << arm32.R4) | (1 << arm32.R5) | (1 << arm32.R6) | (1 << arm32.PC)
}
